# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PRODUCTION environment contract. Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real
# values. `.env*` is gitignored — never commit real credentials.
#
# THIS FILE DESCRIBES THE PRODUCTION SIDE. Its counterpart is
# `.env.local.example`, which describes the non-production side and is copied
# to `.env.test.local`. Which file a runner reads decides which database it
# writes to, so the split is worth stating twice:
#
#   .env.local        production. The app, `npm run dev`, and the ops scripts
#                     — including `scripts/reset-platform.mjs`.
#   .env.test.local   the local Supabase stack. `npm run seed`,
#                     `npm run test:e2e`, `npm run test:integration`, and
#                     `node supabase/tests/run.mjs`.
#
# The separation is enforced, not merely documented: `lib/env/testDatabase.ts`
# throws unless the URL a test runner resolves is a stack on this machine, and
# it throws at config time, before a single test is collected. The escape
# hatch (`ALLOW_REMOTE_TEST_DB=1`) is per-invocation and is set nowhere in this
# repository.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase project URL. Safe to expose to the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Anon (publishable) key. Safe to expose — every query it makes is RLS-bound.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key

# Public origin this deployment is reached on, no trailing slash.
#
# This is what magic links are told to come back to. Supabase checks it against
# the project's redirect allow-list (Dashboard -> Authentication -> URL
# Configuration) and, on a miss, silently redirects to the project Site URL
# instead of erroring — the link still works, it just lands somewhere that
# cannot exchange it. So this value and the allow-list entry must agree exactly.
#
# Leave unset for local work and the request's Host header is used. When testing
# from a phone that Host is a LAN address or a tunnel URL, which is almost never
# allow-listed — set it explicitly and add the same value to the dashboard.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Service-role key. Bypasses RLS. SERVER-ONLY.
# Must be a different secret from the anon key above, and must NEVER be given a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — that would inline it into the client bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# Resend API key for transactional email. Only consulted when EMAIL_DRY_RUN=off.
#
# It serves TWO senders after the M5 cutover:
#   1. `lib/email/sendEmail.ts`, over the Resend HTTP API (the eight in-app
#      templates).
#   2. Supabase's own mailer, over Resend SMTP (the magic link) — configured in
#      the hosted project's dashboard, mirrored in supabase/config.toml.
# One key, one verified domain, one place a deliverability problem can be.
#
# Resend SMTP, for the dashboard fields: smtp.resend.com : 587,
# username `resend`, password THIS key.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_api_key

# Sender identity used for outbound transactional mail. Must be on the domain
# verified in Resend, or every send is rejected.
EMAIL_FROM="Hraj Fotbal <noreply@hrajfotbal.com>"

# Email delivery gate. Anything other than off/false/0/no means DRY RUN:
# payloads are logged, never sent. Unset also means dry run (fail-safe).
#
# PRODUCTION AT LAUNCH: EMAIL_DRY_RUN=off
#
# Flip it only at the M5 cutover and only once Resend's DNS shows verified. A
# half-verified domain does not bounce — it lands quietly in spam, and from
# this side that is indistinguishable from working.
#
# The default direction matters more than the value: a missing or unrecognised
# setting LOGS rather than SENDS (`isDryRun()` in lib/email/sendEmail.ts). A
# misconfigured environment should fail toward silence, never toward mailing
# real players a test run.
#
# The E2E suite forces this to `on` for its own server regardless of what is
# set here, so the sweep specs cannot mail anyone after the cutover. Since
# Phase 1 of v1.3 it also ASSERTS the forced value and refuses to start the
# harness if anything resolved it back to a sending configuration — forcing a
# value and verifying the force are different guarantees, and only the second
# survives someone editing the line that does the forcing.
#
# `off` belongs in exactly one file on exactly one machine: the production
# `.env.local`. No non-production configuration sets it, and nothing in a
# session — no cookie, no query parameter, no admin control — reads or writes
# it: `isDryRun()` consults `process.env` only, which is fixed at server start.
EMAIL_DRY_RUN=on

# IBAN encoded into the Czech SPD 1.0 payment QR strings.
PAYMENT_IBAN=CZ0000000000000000000000

# Shared secret gating the Vercel Cron routes. Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
CRON_SECRET=your-cron-secret
